<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Work hydration principles">
  <div><strong>Access first</strong><span>Cool potable water should be easy to reach rather than saved for the end of the shift.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Heat needs controls</strong><span>Rest, cooling, acclimatization and work scheduling matter alongside drinking.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Electrolytes have a role</strong><span>NIOSH recommends balanced electrolyte drinks when sweating is prolonged for several hours, not as a blanket requirement for every worker.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Shift design</p><h2>Do not make hydration depend on remembering after you are already depleted</h2><p>Long shifts can combine physical work, protective clothing, warm environments, limited break opportunities and inconsistent access to water. The most useful intervention is often operational: put fluids close to the work, make breaks realistic and train workers and supervisors to recognize heat strain.</p><p>For hot work, NIOSH recommends frequent small amounts of cool water, regular rest and a structured acclimatization process. These are workplace heat controls, not product dosing instructions.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/4480799/pexels-photo-4480799.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Worker walking through a large industrial warehouse" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Not every long shift is hot</p><h2>Duration, workload and environment matter more than the clock alone</h2><p>A twelve-hour desk, control-room or driving shift can create a very different fluid problem from eight hours of heavy outdoor labour. Long duration can make drinking less convenient, while physical work and heat directly increase sweat loss.</p><p>Plan the beverage around the actual task. For cool sedentary work, water and meals may be entirely adequate. For prolonged sweating, a balanced carbohydrate-electrolyte beverage may become more useful.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Build the shift plan</p><h2>Four controls before choosing a drink</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Potable water</h3><p>Make safe, cool water available near the work area rather than relying on distant refill points.</p></article><article><h3>Rest and cooling</h3><p>Use shade, air conditioning, ventilation or other appropriate cooling controls when heat exposure is significant.</p></article><article><h3>Acclimatization</h3><p>New and returning workers need gradual exposure to hot environments. NIOSH describes a 7 to 14 day acclimatization process.</p></article><article><h3>Buddy awareness</h3><p>Workers and supervisors should recognize dizziness, weakness, confusion and other heat-illness warning signs and know when to stop work.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where Best Hydrate may fit</p><h2>A prepared option for prolonged sweating and demanding days</h2><p>Best Hydrate can provide a measured source of fluid, glucose and listed electrolytes when mixed according to its label. That may suit hot work, outdoor labour, PPE-heavy tasks or other shifts where sweating continues for hours.</p><p>It should not be used to imply that ordinary water is inadequate for all workers, or that a hydration product can make an unsafe heat exposure safe. Work-rest schedules, cooling, acclimatization and emergency response remain primary controls.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Workplace safety</span><h3>Hydration does not replace heat controls.</h3><p>If a job requires excessive fluid just to remain tolerable, the exposure itself deserves attention.</p></aside></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">After the shift</p><h2>Recovery depends on what the job took out</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3"><article><h3>Heavy sweat loss</h3><p>Rehydrate alongside normal food and sodium intake, especially when another hot shift follows soon.</p><a href="/pages/recovery-hydration">Recovery →</a></article><article><h3>Travel between sites</h3><p>Carry a refillable bottle and plan safe water access when the workday includes long drives or flights.</p><a href="/pages/travel-hydration">Travel →</a></article><article><h3>Routine workday</h3><p>For ordinary conditions, keep water available and let regular meals do much of the electrolyte replacement.</p><a href="/pages/everyday-wellness">Everyday Wellness →</a></article></div></section>

